freestanding-lgrt
=================

freestanding-lgrt, the freestanding LLVM and GCC runtime, is an easy to
integrate implementation of the libgcc-compatible routines that GCC and Clang
call for operations a target can't do inline, in a single C file.

It covers the integer routines, the atomics, the architecture-specific helpers,
and the functions of C23's stdbit.h. The atomic routines exist only where the
compiler implements pointer-sized atomic operations inline.

Supported targets are ELF ones, built with GCC 4.7 or later or Clang 6 or
later, for x86-64 (including x32), IA-32, aarch64, 32-bit ARM (ARMv4T to
ARMv8.1-M), 32- and 64-bit RISC-V, 32- and 64-bit LoongArch, MIPS, PowerPC,
s390x, SPARC, Alpha, PA-RISC, m68k, SuperH, Hexagon, and ARC, plus 16-bit x86
code built with -m16 (GCC, or Clang 12 or later).

To use it, compile src/freestanding-lgrt.c with the same compiler and flags as
the rest of the program, but without LTO (-fno-lto), and link the object in.
The program must provide memcpy, memmove, memset, and memcmp. Compilers other
than GCC and Clang stop the build with an error.

Known compiler issues:

- Clang 12 at -Oz miscompiles code that its machine outliner factors out for
  ARM M-profile targets: build with -mno-outline there.
- On MIPS64, and on SPARC64 with Clang before 21, GCC and Clang pass __int128
  arguments differently: build the library with the same compiler as the code
  that calls its 16-byte atomic routines.
